WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.837
actress and singer you know from
shows like "Game of Thrones,"

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.373
"Sex Education,"
and ""Ted Lasso.

00:00:06.373 --> 00:00:10.009
Her special "Home for Christmas"
is streaming now on Apple TV+.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.410
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.813
-♪ Everybody's waitin'
for the man with the bag ♪

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:17.250
♪ 'Cause Christmas
is here again ♪

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.486
♪ He'll be here ♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.121
♪ With the answers
to the prayers ♪

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:23.923
♪ That you've made
through the year ♪

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:26.826
♪ You'll get yours ♪

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:29.395
♪ If you've done
everything you should ♪

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.030
♪ Extra special good ♪

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.099
♪ He'll make this December ♪

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.833
♪ The one you'll remember ♪

00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:38.138
♪ The best and the merriest
you ever did have ♪

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:42.041
-Please welcome back to the show
Hannah Waddingham, everybody!

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.810
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.814
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:47.814 --> 00:01:02.361
♪♪

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:04.530
Welcome!
-Hello!

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.001
-I'm thrilled to have you here.
-We're in the flesh.

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.635
-We're in the flesh.
-Ooh!

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.837
-We're in the flesh.
We've done this once over Zoom.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:13.472
-Yes.
-I did not realize

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.942
that I had actually seen you
in the flesh before, though,

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.378
because I saw you
onstage in "Spamalot."

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:22.548
-"Spamalot."
-Now back onstage,

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.317
a revival.
-Yeah, I'm going to try

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.319
and go and check it out,
because, I mean,

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.855
those were happy, happy,
exhausting vocal days.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.158
-Yeah!
-You've never seen --

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.727
The Lady of the Lake
is a exhausting role.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.796
-Oh, my God,
it is completely exhausting.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.565
But don't tell
the producers this.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:40.232
I 100% would have done it
for no money.

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:42.735
Some of the greatest days
of my life.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.037
And Mr. Tim Curry.
-Mr. Tim Curry, I mean...

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:47.039
-King Arthur. It was fantastic.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.608
-Had the audience
in the palm of his hand

00:01:49.608 --> 00:01:51.244
from the first second.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.878
-Now, I've heard great things
about the revival.

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:55.782
Have you gone and seen shows
that you had been in originally?

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.550
Have you ever done this before,
where you go...

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.686
-Uh, no -- Yeah,
I have done a little bit,

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.654
but this one,
I've been kind of avoiding.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.722
But I just think, you know,
it started here.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:06.492
So I want to go back and
support this revival, you know,

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:10.262
and Eric Idle and I are
absolutely friends forever.

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:11.730
So, yeah, I need to go
and check it out

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.332
and take my little girl,
who I have here with me.

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:14.700
-Very exciting.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.902
You have your little girl
in New York City for Christmas?

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:17.770
Is this is her first
Christmas in the city?

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.706
-Yes, it is.
-How is she liking it so far?

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:22.374
-She honestly -- I think my
brother that's come with us --

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:24.677
I think he's actually
even more excited.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.312
Yesterday my brother was like,
"This is where they shot 'Elf!'"

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:29.415
And my daughter was like,
"Okay,"

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.184
because she's been here
quite a lot, you know?

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:34.020
So, yeah, my 52-year-old brother
was losing his mind.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.855
-I remember, in
your dressing room,

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:38.891
he's like, "Have you met Elf?"
-Yeah, yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.927
-Do you, uh --
But I would imagine

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.695
London is lovely
at Christmastime.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.363
-Oh, it's gorg-- but it's
a totally different vibe.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.698
-Yeah.
-Here,

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.634
there's just something --
I was saying to my driver,

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.535
I was just like, "Here,
it doesn't matter.

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:53.372
It's like dirty bins,
but Christmas stuff, as well.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:56.775
So you're like, "It's gritty.
It's New York."

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:58.844
-Sometimes, if
you're lucky enough,

00:02:58.844 --> 00:03:01.347
a rat will run by
with a little sack of presents.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.182
-Yeah, and some...
-Yeah.

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:05.250
-...little antlers going on.
-A lot of little antlers.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.052
-Do you know what it is?
It's cute.

00:03:07.052 --> 00:03:10.456
-Yeah, Mayor Adams has
an Antlers on Rats initiative.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.791
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:14.294
It's very exciting.
You -- I also heard --

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.794
I mean, I think everybody
in the building heard --

00:03:15.794 --> 00:03:16.896
It was very exciting.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:18.330
It was an "SNL,"
the Christmas episode.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.066
Kate McKinnon was there.
But not just you,

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.834
but the whole "Ted Lasso"
cast was there.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.802
-Yeah, we travel
in a nauseating pack.

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:25.905
-Yes It's really fun.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.807
I mean, we obviously saw
they were -- a bunch of them

00:03:28.807 --> 00:03:30.576
were in your clip there
from your Christmas special.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:32.244
-Yeah.
-But I think sometimes --

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:33.679
you know, again, all I've done,

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.915
all my casts in my life,
have been, like,

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.483
the live cast where I actually
spent all week together.

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.017
But you guys don't actually see
each other that much.

00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:40.753
-No, we don't.
So, when we do,

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.355
we're just
like, "Nah, nah, nah."

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.857
We just -- I mean, sometimes
I think people think

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:47.026
it's contractual
that we spend time together.

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:48.394
You know, must maintain that.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.130
But we just -- it's like
a feeding frenzy.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.100
We just can't -- Even things
where we do Q&amp;A's and stuff,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.768
we're trying
to slip out the door

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.270
to start our evening together,
which is so lovely.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.639
And I think that comes
through the camera

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:02.741
and that's why people have loved
Ted so much.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:04.243
-Yeah, I think
that's really true.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.045
-Yeah, we miss it.
-You do get that camaraderie.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.880
-We miss it, Jason!
-Yeah!

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.650
-We miss it.
-We all miss it, Jason!

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.785
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:15.254
Um, you got
the "Ted Lasso" cast job.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:16.723
You also got
another guest star,

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:19.225
which I heard was
your daughter's insistence.

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.127
-Yeah.
I mean, when I said to her

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.029
I wanted to give her
the opportunity

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.596
to choose one of my guests
for my special,

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:26.164
I mean, that could
have gone horribly wrong.

00:04:26.164 --> 00:04:28.667
I could have had, you know,
like a purple alligator

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.670
or something
turn up in a costume.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.772
And she literally,
before I finished my sentence,

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.040
just went,
"Leslie Odom Jr., Mummy!"

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.376
So I was just like,
"I don't think

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.646
we'll be able to get him
because he's probably busy

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:42.614
doing a show.
But the call went out.

00:04:42.614 --> 00:04:45.918
And you know, of all my guests,
I have to say,

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.654
I think his and my voices
really meld together.

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.690
Although I did, you know --
I know it was my special,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.658
but I did have to think --
you literally --

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:54.560
like a competition-winner
Hannah.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.662
When I had my microphone,
I was like...

00:04:56.662 --> 00:04:58.398
[ Sings indistinctly ]

00:04:58.398 --> 00:04:59.965
'Cause he's --
♪ he's dreamy ♪

00:04:59.965 --> 00:05:02.568
-He is dreamy.
Now, do you attribute this

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.437
to your daughter
just having a very good ear

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.105
and saying, "I think
Leslie Odom Jr.

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:07.406
would be a good fit"?
-Yeah, because

00:05:07.406 --> 00:05:10.475
you have to think of kind of
the shape of the show

00:05:10.475 --> 00:05:14.280
and the vocal quality
of different people with you.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.681
You know, Luke Evans
that I sing with,

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:17.950
I've known him
for 20 years in theater.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:21.921
Sam Ryder, a great talent from
the U.K., from our Eurovision.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:24.323
And so I wanted somebody
that I knew

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:26.625
was both Broadway feeding into,

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.327
you know, the stuff
that I've done here,

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:31.029
but also, you know,
with his own stuff out there.

00:05:31.029 --> 00:05:35.001
And honestly, for Apple to have
given me the luxury of going,

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.269
"I would like this person,
this person, this person,

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:38.837
and I want it to be
at the London Coliseum"

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:41.507
was just dreamy.
-And so the London Coliseum

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:42.942
is a very important building
to you,

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.243
a very important theater.
-Yeah.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.045
-So was it both
your parents performed there?

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:48.180
-No, no, my mum, my mum. Yeah.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.249
Both her parents
were opera singers,

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:52.718
and she was a principal at
Covent Garden before I was born.

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.587
And then she spent 30 years

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:56.389
in the English National Opera
Chorus.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.223
So I was there
from the age of 8,

00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:00.392
sitting in
that beautiful theater.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.027
And then, when we shot it,

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:04.597
my daughter was 8 at the time,
in May.

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:08.300
She was sitting in the box
that I sat in from the age of 8

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.736
until my mom retired
when I was 38.

00:06:10.736 --> 00:06:12.137
-Wow.
-Which was insane.

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:14.406
And then my mom there --
bless her -- both my parents,

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:17.377
who were so heavily inflicted
with terrible illness,

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.812
my mum still with
fighting Parkinson's,

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.548
but they were there
at the back of the auditorium.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:25.217
So the whole show is really
a love letter to her

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:26.518
and to them
and that building.

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:29.188
Yeah, because I wanted
it to have,

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:32.124
-- you know,
it had to have real heart.

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.093
The other Christmas specials
that there are,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:35.962
you know, I have
no delusions of grandeur

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:37.797
that I come from
a very different world.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:40.032
So I wanted to have
completely live vocals

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:42.001
that nobody has touched,
nobody's tweaked.

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:45.371
Everything was live
in that killer environment.

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:48.273
So, yeah, it's my little
Christmas kiss to everyone.

00:06:48.273 --> 00:06:50.976
-Do a lot of opera singers
have opera singers as parents?

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:52.544
Is this something
that is passed down?

00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:53.980
-I don't know,
but I know that I've had

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.216
a lot of judgment in my time,
even when I was doing this.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.517
-Really?
-Yeah, my mum.

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:01.654
Like, my dad kind of
gives me a rib crusher.

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:04.590
My mom's just like,
doesn't that hurt?

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.792
You know, the kind of
the shouty singing.

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.461
-Oh, so"Doesn't that hurt?"
is not actual sympathy.

00:07:09.461 --> 00:07:11.330
-Oh, no, no, no,
it's a way of saying.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.799
"You should be singing properly
like I know you can."

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:15.501
-"Doesn't it hurt
to do it wrong?"

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:16.936
-Yeah, "Doesn't it hurt?

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:19.005
Wouldn't you rather
sound like me?"

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:20.940
And my dad was just like --

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.375
He said nothing
on my Broadway debut.

00:07:23.375 --> 00:07:25.278
And then, the next night,
rib crusher

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:27.012
and went, "Much better
than last night."

00:07:27.012 --> 00:07:28.514
-Oh, wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:30.316
-"Love you."
-That's pretty good.

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:32.718
I mean, the fact
that he sat on it

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:34.220
for a night
is very sweet.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.722
-No, don't give him that.
-Okay, I won't.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:39.257
You also recently sang
"O Holy Night"

00:07:39.257 --> 00:07:41.527
in front of Prince William
and Kate Middleton.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:43.696
More nerves, less nerves,
or at this point,

00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:46.165
are you beyond nerves?
-Oh, no, I'm absolutely --

00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:48.600
No, I think it gets worse.
The more established you get,

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.235
you feel like people
are like, "Okay."

00:07:50.235 --> 00:07:52.638
-Yeah, yeah.
-But the thing that was lovely,

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:56.175
I've just hosted the
Earthshot Environmental Awards

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.743
for Prince William,
out in Singapore,

00:07:57.743 --> 00:08:00.346
with lovely Sterling K. Brown.
-Oh, he's great.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:02.381
-Yeah, he and I got on
like a house on fire.

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:05.651
So, it was the second time
I'd seen Prince William.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.320
And I did say to him in the kind
of receiving line afterwards,

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.323
I was like, "Sir, I don't know
whether you're stalking me

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.990
or I'm stalking you."
-Uh-huh.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.260
-And he was just like
[Chuckles].

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.795
[ Laughter ]

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:17.763
Probably shouldn't say
the word "stalking" to a royal.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:20.465
-Yeah. I was going to say,
"And then security tackled you."

00:08:20.465 --> 00:08:23.402
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-I want to congratulate you.

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:26.838
Not only another Emmy nomination
for "Ted Lasso"

00:08:26.838 --> 00:08:29.141
but a Golden Globe!
Golden Globe nomination!

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:31.510
-Yeah! Come on, the gala! Yes!
-Very exciting.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.780
-I cannot be cool about it
at all.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.215
-I love that you
can't be cool about.

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.416
-I'm absolutely not cool at all,

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:38.717
because of the people
in the category.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:39.985
-I hate when people
get nominated

00:08:39.985 --> 00:08:41.320
and are like, "You know what?
It's just an honor

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.788
to do the work."
You're like, "Come on."

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:45.558
-No, it's not.
Am I allowed to swear?

00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:47.727
-Yeah. Go [Bleep].
-[Bleep] epic.

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:48.961
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:50.496
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:53.132
-You know what's great?
When people curse

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:54.967
in a British accent,
we don't even have to beep it.

00:08:54.967 --> 00:08:57.803
-No, just sounds more classy.
-It just sounds classy,

00:08:57.803 --> 00:09:00.372
the way you say it.
You have some competition.

00:09:00.372 --> 00:09:03.575
-There's a very little-known
actress in my category --

00:09:03.575 --> 00:09:05.544
and I hope she's going to
do great things --

00:09:05.544 --> 00:09:07.279
called Meryl Streep.
Yes.

00:09:07.279 --> 00:09:08.981
-I feel like
that's very exciting.

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:11.250
That's almost like
an extra Emmy nomination

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.452
if you find yourself
into a category with Streep.

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:15.721
The Globes or the Emmys.
-It's the greatest win-win,

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:18.156
because if she doesn't win,
hilariously,

00:09:18.156 --> 00:09:19.658
then you're like, "Hello,

00:09:19.658 --> 00:09:21.493
I'm in a losing category
with Meryl Streep."

00:09:21.493 --> 00:09:24.596
But if she does, you're like,
"Guys, Meryl Streep won."

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:26.599
-You're leaving out
the third option --

00:09:26.599 --> 00:09:28.667
you win and then,
the whole night,

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.469
you're like,
"I'm better than Meryl."

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:33.739
-[Bleep] epic!
-Forever!

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.173
[ Laughter ]

00:09:35.173 --> 00:09:37.075
